# NodePagesWithoutProvenance.allow — the node-landing-page provenance ratchet (#4500).
#
# Each line is a production file that registers a renderer for an area name that is SOMEBODY's
# landing page, using the bare `WithView` / `WithNamedRenderer` rather than
# `MeshNodeLayoutAreas.WithNodePage`, and therefore records no NodePageProvenance verdict:
#
#     layout.WithView(MeshNodeLayoutAreas.SearchArea, Search)
#
# WHY THE SHAPE IS A DEFECT. The `Type · Created · Updated` line rode on ONE renderer — the
# framework's Overview — and `WithNamedRenderer` is last-writer-wins. A node type that registered
# its own landing page replaced that renderer and the provenance line went with it, with nothing
# anywhere recording the loss. Measured 2026-09-16: of the 99 landing pages across the fleet that
# replace the framework renderer, 86 had lost it — 4 here, 82 in MeshWeaver.Plugins. An omission
# somebody had WEIGHED (an Activity page reports its own run's start and end) and one nobody had
# thought about were the same observation from outside: an absence.
#
# THE FIX AT A SITE is `WithNodePage(area, page)`. With no second argument the framework composes
# the line above the page, so a landing page written without an opinion ships correct. A page that
# already draws the standard header passes `NodePageProvenance.RenderedByThePage`; one that should
# carry none passes `NodePageProvenance.Declined("<why>")` — and the reason is mandatory, because a
# blank decline records that somebody decided without recording what.
#
# WHY ENTRIES EXIST AT ALL. The scanner cannot tell which hub a registration lands on, and an area
# name is a landing page on one hub and an ordinary tab on another: `Search` is `Partition`'s
# landing page (registered there through `WithNodePage`, in PartitionNodeType) and every other
# node's search TAB. Flagging by name over-flags on purpose — under-flagging costs a page nobody
# notices is missing something, which is the whole issue — so the judgement lives here, per file,
# in a sentence.
#
# THIS RATCHET MAY ONLY SHRINK. A new file, or a raised count, is a failure; so is a count left
# HIGHER than the tree (a stale entry reds, so the reason beside it is re-read when it stops being
# true). Delete an entry when it reaches 0.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, and why that is not a gap to close here: `src/` only. Most of the fleet's
# landing pages live in in-mesh `Source/*.cs` and in NodeType `configuration` lambdas that compile
# at RUNTIME in the portal — invisible to every compiler and every scan in this repository. Those
# pages are reached by the changed DEFAULT, not by this guard. Widening the roots would produce a
# guard that scans a tree the defect does not live in.
#
# Format: relative/path<TAB>count. `#` starts a comment.

# The framework's own Search TAB, registered for every node hub by AddDefaultLayoutAreas. It is a
# landing page on exactly one hub — Partition — and PartitionNodeType re-registers it there through
# WithNodePage (node-type configuration runs AFTER the default chain), so the Partition landing page
# does carry a verdict. This registration is the tab, on every other node in the mesh.
src/MeshWeaver.Graph/MeshNodeLayoutAreas.cs	1

# The NodeType page's instance-search TAB, wrapped in the NodeType shell. A NodeType node's landing
# page is Overview, registered immediately above it through WithNodePage with RenderedByThePage
# (NodeTypeLayoutAreas.OverviewContent calls MeshNodeLayoutAreas.BuildHeader).
src/MeshWeaver.Graph/NodeTypeLayoutAreas.cs	1
